/-!
# Signal Double Ratchet with XOR encryption: Extraction to Security

Instantiates the hax-extracted `DoubleRatchetDeps` of the crate `doubleratchet-hax`
(<https://github.com/spitters/signal-hax>) with libcrux-specs hash primitives, an X25519 function
`sm` and XOR in place of the AEAD, and carries the secure-messaging game of the extracted session
functions at this instance to the Signal double ratchet of `Signal.DoubleRatchet_UC`.

The trait `DoubleRatchetDeps` of the crate is a class over a phantom type `Self`. The type
`DrPrims sm` carries the instance `doubleratchetDepsOf sm` of the primitives at the X25519
function `sm`: `hmac_sha256` is `hmac_sha256_1` on the decoded key and the one-byte message,
`hkdf_extract` is `hkdf_extract_32`, `hkdf_expand_64` is `hkdf_expand_64` on the decoded PRK and
the 14-byte info string, and `x25519` is `sm`. The decodings `decodeVec` invert `vecToArrayInt`.
The chain step of the crate is HMAC-SHA256 of the chain key on the seeds `ck_const 1` (message
key) and `ck_const 2` (next chain key); the root step is HKDF-SHA256 with the root key as salt
and the Diffie–Hellman output as input keying material, expanded to 64 bytes under
`"WhisperRatchet"` and split into the next root key and the chain key.

Encryption is XOR: `aead_encrypt` is `xorBytes` of the decoded key and the text, whatever the
associated data, and `aead_decrypt` is always `some` of the `xorBytes` at the plaintext length
`32`. The AEAD of the model, `libcruxAEAD`, is the XOR scheme with the matching decryption; the
core scheme `Signal.FSAEAD.toAEAD` has a decryption that always rejects and cannot satisfy the
decryption equation `aead_dec_eq` of `DrHom`. The theorems of this file are statements about
confidentiality at this instance: the decryption accepts every ciphertext, the games deliver no
ciphertext to a receiver, and the AEAD reduction `fsCorrAEADRed` asks at most one encryption
query and no decryption query, so that the AEAD term of the bounds is that of a one-time pad.

The Diffie–Hellman CKA of the model is over the prime-order subgroup `G25519 B` of Curve25519
generated by a point `B` of order `ℓ`, with exponents `ZMod ℓ` and elements encoded by their
u-coordinate `encU`. The hypothesis is `X25519HomOf sm bp B`, the RFC 7748 correctness of `sm`
on that subgroup. At the libcrux X25519 it is `X25519Hom B`; at the specification
`Spec.x25519_spec` and the base point `B9` it is the theorem `X25519HomOf.spec_B9`. The model
draws the secret key `(sk, b)` of `Sk = Vector UInt8 32 × Bool` with the exponent
`signedE (sk, b) = ± clampE sk`; the law `signedClamp` of the signed clamped scalar is within
`Δ25519 ≤ 2^-126` of uniform on `ZMod ℓ` (`signedClampCert`).

`signalDR_extracted_corrupt_advantage_le` and `signalDR_extracted_adaptive_advantage_le`: the
session functions of the crate at `DrPrims x25519_scalarmult` as the secure-messaging scheme
`signalDRLibcruxSM B` of `ExtractedScheme`, in the ACD corruption game with forward secrecy and
post-compromise security and in the game with an adaptive challenge epoch. Under `X25519Hom B`
the scheme simulates the ACD construction with headers over the Diffie–Hellman CKA
`dhCKAOf (G25519 B) signedE`; the bounds are those of `drExtracted_corruptAdvantage_le` and
`drExtracted_adaptiveAdvantage_le` at explicit reductions of the encoded adversary, plus
`4 Δ25519` for each CKA term of the corruption game at `signedClampCert`.
`signalDR_spec_corrupt_advantage_le` is the bound of the corruption game for
`DrPrims Spec.x25519_spec` with the CKA over `G25519 B9`, without an X25519 hypothesis. These
schemes are instances of `drExtractedSM`, the store-free abstraction of the ratchet: a corruption
returns the key material `drExpose`. The bound of the CKA terms by DDH holds for `2 ≤ ΔPCS`.
`signalDR_extracted_adaptive_advantage_le_tsum` is stated for `1 ≤ ΔPCS` with the CKA advantage as
a term; at `ΔPCS = 1` the party that sent in the epoch before the challenge still holds the
exponent that determines the challenge key, and that term is not bounded by DDH.

For passive adversaries asking at most `q` queries, the instances
`signalDRLibcrux_passive_gameAdvBoundC` and `signalDRSpec_passive_gameAdvBoundC` bound the
advantage of every post-processing of the output bit of the game with an adaptive challenge
epoch by `signalDRPassiveBound`; the instances at the named games
`signalDRLibcruxPassiveReal`, `signalDRLibcruxPassiveIdeal`, `signalDRSpecPassiveReal` and
`signalDRSpecPassiveIdeal`, indexed by `Unit` for a fixed adversary, give the concrete UC form
`UCFromGameC` by instance search.

## References

* J. Alwen, S. Coretti, Y. Dodis, "The Double Ratchet: Security Notions, Proofs, and
  Modularization for the Signal Protocol", Eurocrypt 2019.
* T. Perrin, M. Marlinspike, "The Double Ratchet Algorithm", Signal specification, 2016.
-/

